brief_summary:
The goal of this observational study is to identify factors associated with the occurrence of grade 2 or higher radiation dermatitis in adults with head and neck cancer treated with radiotherapy who receive preventive photobiomodulation (PBM) as part of their standard care.

The main question it aims to answer is: which clinical, demographic, tumor-related, treatment-related and functional factors are associated with grade 2 or higher radiation dermatitis (NCI-CTCAE v5.0) despite preventive PBM?

Participants will receive their usual radiotherapy and their usual preventive PBM sessions (3 sessions per week throughout radiotherapy). In addition, for the purpose of the study, they will:

* perform a 1-minute sit-to-stand test and a handgrip strength measurement at inclusion and at the end of radiotherapy;
* complete the EORTC QLQ-H\&N35 quality-of-life questionnaire before radiotherapy, at mid-treatment and at the end of radiotherapy;
* have standardized photographs taken of the irradiated skin. Skin toxicity will be graded at each PBM session.

The goal of this clinical trial is to learn whether the ultrasound-guided GENIVS block is not worse than (non-inferior to) the adductor canal block in reducing opioid use after total knee arthroplasty performed under spinal anesthesia. The main question it aims to answer is:

\- Is total opioid consumption (intravenous tramadol, mg) in the first 24 hours after surgery with the GENIVS block not clinically worse than with the adductor canal block?

Researchers will also compare pain scores, time to the first analgesic request, the need for rescue pain medication, mobilization (Timed Up and Go test and stair climbing), quality of recovery (QoR-15) and opioid-related side effects between the two groups.

Participants will:

* have elective, primary, one-sided knee replacement surgery under spinal anesthesia;
* be randomly assigned to receive either a GENIVS block or an adductor canal block, with the same local anesthetic dose, at the end of surgery;
* use an intravenous tramadol patient-controlled analgesia pump and receive standard pain treatment for 72 hours;
* rate their pain and complete mobility tests and questionnaires at scheduled times during the first 72 hours after surgery.
